{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"**Di­atoni­c chords in G major**\r\n\r\n| Scale degree | Roman numeral | Chord (quality) | Common open/box shape (frets) |\r\n|--------------|---------------|----------------|------------------------------|\r\n| I            | I             | **G maj**      | 3‑2‑0‑0‑0‑3 |\r\n| ii           | ii            | **A min**      | x‑0‑2‑2‑1‑0 |\r\n| iii          | iii           | **B min**      | x‑2‑4‑4‑3‑2 |\r\n| IV           | IV            | **C maj**      | x‑3‑2‑0‑1‑0 |\r\n| V            | V             | **D maj**      | x‑x‑0‑2‑3‑2 |\r\n| vi           | vi            | **E min**      | 0‑2‑2‑0‑0‑0 |\r\n| vii°         | vii°          | **F♯ dim**     | 2‑x‑2‑3‑2‑x |\r\n\r\nBelow are the exact tab shapes for each chord (low E = string 6, high E = string 1).\r\n\r\n**G maj**  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (3/6.2/5.0/4.0/3.0/2.0/1)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**A min**  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (0/5.0/4.2/3.2/2.1/1)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**B min**  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (2/5.x/4.2/3.4/2.3/1.2)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**C maj**  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (3/5.x/4.3/3.2/2.1/1)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**D maj**  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (2/5.x/4.x/3.0/2.3/1.2)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**E min**  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (0/6.0/5.2/4.2/3.0/2.0/1)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**F♯ dim**  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (2/6.2/x/4.3/3.2/1.x)\r\n```\r\n\r\nThese seven chords are the diatonic set you can use for progressions in the key of G major (e.g., I–IV–V, ii–V–I, vi–ii–V, etc.). Let me know if you’d like specific voicings, inversions, or common progressions built from them!","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":3337,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":14,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":0,"id":"f-37","kind":null,"label":"skill.diatonicchords","match":null,"prompt":"what chords are in the key of G","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":