{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"The two notes that belong to **both** D major (D‑F♯‑A) and B minor (B‑D‑F♯) are **D** and **F♯**.  \r\nIf you want a smooth voice‑leading move, keep one of those tones ringing as you change the other fingers.\r\n\r\n**Common‑tone options**\r\n\r\n| Common tone | How to keep it ringing on guitar |\r\n|-------------|-----------------------------------|\r\n| **D** (open 4th string) | Play the D‑major shape **xx0232** and let the open D string ring while you switch the other fingers to a Bm shape. |\r\n| **F♯** (2nd‑string 2nd fret) | Play D‑major **xx0232** and leave the 2nd‑string finger on the 2nd fret as you move to a Bm shape that also uses that F♯ (e.g., **x20202**). |\r\n\r\n**One practical voicing pair**\r\n\r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (2/4.2/3.0/2)   // D major: xx0232 (rings D on the 4th string)\r\n```\r\n\r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (2/5.2/4.0/3)   // B minor: x20202 (keeps the F♯ on the 2nd string)\r\n```\r\n\r\nSo you can either let the **open D** (4th string) continue or keep the **F♯** on the 2nd‑string 2nd fret while moving from D → Bm for a seamless transition.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":2119,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":3,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":1,"id":"f-40","kind":null,"label":"skill.commontones","match":null,"prompt":"going from D to Bm, which note can i leave ringing","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"F","verified":2}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"The only pitch that belongs to both an **A‑major (A C♯ E)** and a **D‑major (D F♯ A)** triad is **A**.  \r\n\r\n▶ Use A as a sustained drone under both chords. On guitar the most convenient drones are:\r\n\r\n* Open A string (5th string)\r\n* 5th fret on the low E string (E → A)\r\n\r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (0/5)       // open A string (5th string)\r\n```\r\n\r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (5/6)       // 5th fret, low E string (A)\r\n```\r\n\r\nFeel free to layer the drone with the chord voicings—A will be the root of the A‑major chord and the fifth of the D‑major chord, giving a strong tonal anchor for both.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":1855,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":3,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":2,"id":"f-48","kind":null,"label":"skill.commontones","match":null,"prompt":"writing a drone part under two chords, what notes are in both A and D","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"F","verified":2}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"**Transposed progression (down 2 half‑steps):**  \r\n\r\n**Ab – Fm – Db – Eb**\r\n\r\nBelow are practical, guitar‑friendly shapes you can use right away.  \r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### 1. Ab major (E‑shape barre at the 4th fret)  \r\n\r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (4/6.6/5.6/4.5/3.4/2.4/1)\r\n```  \r\n\r\n*Tip:* Let the index finger bar the 4th fret, ring finger on the A‑string 6th fret, and pinky on the D‑string 6th fret.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### 2. Fm minor (E‑shape barre at the 1st fret)  \r\n\r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (1/6.3/5.3/4.4.2/3.1/2.1/1)\r\n```  \r\n\r\n(1/6 = low E 1, 3/5 = A 3, 3/4 = D 3, 2/3 = G 2, 1/2 = B 1, 1/1 = high E 1)\r\n\r\n*Tip:* Use a full‑bar with the index finger, then form the open‑E minor shape.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### 3. Db major (A‑shape barre at the 4th fret)  \r\n\r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (4/5.6/4.6/3.4/3.4/2.4/1)\r\n```  \r\n\r\n(4/5 = A 4, 6/4 = D 6, 6/3 = G 6, 5/2 = B 5, 4/1 = high E 4)\r\n\r\n*Tip:* Bar the 4th fret from the A‑string down and shape an open A‑major chord.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### 4. Eb major (E‑shape barre at the 6th fret)  \r\n\r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (6/6.8/5.8/4.7/5/4.6/1)\r\n```  \r\n\r\n(6/6 = low E 6, 8/5 = A 8, 8/4 = D 8, 7/3 = G 7, 6/2 = B 6, 6/1 = high E 6)\r\n\r\n*Tip:* Same shape as the Ab chord but moved up two frets.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Quick capo alternative  \r\n\r\nIf you prefer open‑chord shapes, place a **capo on the 4th fret** and play the familiar open shapes:\r\n\r\n- **Ab** → play **G** shape (G major)  \r\n- **Fm** → play **Em** shape (E minor)  \r\n- **Db** → play **C** shape (C major)  \r\n- **Eb** → play **D** shape (D major)\r\n\r\nThis gives the exact same pitch with easier fingerings.\r\n\r\nEnjoy the new key! Let me know if you need a specific voicing or a melodic line for the singer.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":4646,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":1,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":0,"id":"f-93","kind":null,"label":"skill.transpose","match":null,"prompt":"singer wants it 2 half steps lower: Bb Gm Eb F","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"T","verified":1}
{"agent":"theory","answer":"In the key of **E natural minor (Aeolian)** the seven diatonic triads are:\r\n1. **i – E minor** (E G B)\r\n2. **ii° – F♯ diminished** (F♯ A C)\r\n3. **III – G major** (G B D)\r\n4. **iv – A minor** (A C E)\r\n5. **v – B minor** (B D F♯)\r\n6. **VI – C major** (C E G)\r\n7. **VII – D major** (D F♯ A) – often written **♭VII** to distinguish it from the leading‑tone diminished chord that appears in harmonic minor.\r\n\r\n**Why the V is often altered**: In minor keys the natural‑minor VII (♭VII) provides a weak dominant function. By raising the seventh scale degree (D→D♯) to create **E harmonic minor**, the v‑triad becomes a **major V (B major: B D♯ F♯)**, and a new leading‑tone diminished chord **vii° (D♯dim: D♯ F♯ A)** appears. The V (or V⁷) is commonly used because it gives a strong dominant‑tonic resolution.\r\n\r\n**A note on terminology**: The phrase “seven chords” can also refer to the set of diatonic seventh‑chords (e.g., i⁷, iv⁷, etc.). Here we are listing the seven diatonic **triads**; the corresponding seventh‑chord set would be i⁷, iiø⁷, III⁷, iv⁷, V⁷ (or V7 in harmonic minor), VI⁷, and ♭VII⁷.\r\n\r\nThus, the core diatonic triads for E minor are as listed above, with the optional harmonic‑minor alteration (V major and vii°) that is frequently employed for stronger functional harmony.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":12458,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":16,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":0,"id":"f-96","kind":null,"label":"skill.diatonicchords","match":null,"prompt":"list the 7 chords in e minor pls","routing":"llm","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"T","verified":16}
